init.description:
The broker presented a new CA certificate.

Old fingerprint: {old_fingerprint}
New fingerprint: {new_fingerprint}
Subject: {subject}
Issuer: {issuer}
Valid until: {valid_until}
Endpoint: {endpoint}
Detected: {detected_at}

Legitimate causes include a first broker install, a reinstall or loss of /data/mqtt/certs, restoring a backup without certificates, migrating to a new installation, migrating a legacy CA, automatic rotation near expiry, or a manual CA replacement.

If you recognize this change, choose verified. The integration stays active. If you do not recognize it, choose not recognized to review the consequences before disabling anything.

not_recognized.description:
The integration is still active and will continue to communicate with the current broker until it is disabled.

Disabling it now will:
- make all its entities unavailable;
- make automations using them fail or stop acting;
- stop history updates;
- stop device control from Home Assistant;
- keep the entry stopped while disabled.

No data, entities or credentials are deleted. You can re-enable the entry after verification.

Verify the broker fingerprint through a trusted channel. Do not regenerate credentials or reconnect before you have verified the broker.
